WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:08.800
THE KNAWLEDGE COMEBACK:
Remember Tai Lopez?

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.719
He dominated YouTube ads in the mid 2010’s
with his quote on quote KNAWLEDGE.

00:00:12.719 --> 00:00:15.509
Tai’s video format was pretty simple.

00:00:15.509 --> 00:00:19.760
He usually recorded himself in front of flashy
things like lamborghinis, private jets, and

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:20.760
mansions.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:25.670
But, in the video itself, he usually dismisses
these materialistic objects and claims that

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:27.170
true wealth is KNAWLEDGE.

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:31.599
He would go onto claim that you can get this
KNAWLEDGE by buying his extremely cheap courses

00:00:31.599 --> 00:00:34.360
that “only” cost a few thousand dollars
each.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:39.430
While this was pretty simple pitch, it was
extraordinarily effective.

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:43.700
Something about him recording on his phone
and the low production quality gave his videos

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.590
a sense of authenticity.

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:50.170
There was of course people who always hated
this guy and thought that he was a scammer.

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:55.520
But, there’s no denying that his videos
were quite persuasive and hard to ignore.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:59.840
If you go through his comments today, you’ll
see paying saying things like “5 years ago

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:01.940
we were forced to watch this.

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:03.620
Today we look it up and enjoy it.”

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:05.580
“We’ve come full circle.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:10.939
I used to never be able to escape this ad
and here I am years later looking it up.”

00:01:10.939 --> 00:01:14.970
With comments like these, you know it’s
an internet classic.

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:19.900
But despite his popularity in the mid 2010s,
Tai more or less fell out of relevancy in

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:23.130
the later part of the decade and throughout
the 2020s.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:27.720
He never really stopped posting altogether,
but his channel metrics were on a substantial

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:28.720
decline.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:33.570
His subscriber count stalled at 1.3 million
and was actually starting to go down.

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:37.690
Meanwhile, he was getting tens of thousands
of views per video which was terrible for

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:40.759
someone who was once getting millions per
video.

00:01:40.759 --> 00:01:46.939
This was until recently when he hit the YouTube
algorithm once again, and boy oh boy, he hit

00:01:46.939 --> 00:01:48.219
bigger than ever.

00:01:48.219 --> 00:01:54.799
In the last 30 days, Tai was able to pull
in 170 million views and gain over 200,000

00:01:54.799 --> 00:01:56.040
subscribers.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.869
And this wasn’t just a one time fluke either.

00:01:58.869 --> 00:02:00.890
Tai has been pulling these numbers since August.

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:06.729
So, here’s the rise, fall, and rise again
of the most infamous internet marketer of

00:02:06.729 --> 00:02:09.530
all time: Tai Lopez.

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:14.810
SPREADING KNAWLEDGE:
Taking a look back, Tai’s internet journey

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:17.630
starts long before his fame as meme.

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:23.110
In fact, it dates all the way back to 2007
at which point most people would’ve described

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:24.750
him as a bum.

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:29.430
According to Tai himself, he was living in
a trailer park with $47 in his bank account.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:33.540
He didn’t have a college degree, much work
experience, or any connections resulting in

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:35.580
this less than ideal situation.

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:38.000
But, he was determined to make changes.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.290
So, he grabbed a yellow pages book from his
kitchen, went to the finance section, and

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:44.520
looked for the biggest ad on the page.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:49.110
He figured that whoever was paying for this
ad had a lot of money and more importantly

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:50.380
you know knowledge.

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:54.810
So, Tai tracked down the man who was paying
for this ad and it turned out to be some guy

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:56.660
named Mike Stainback.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:00.720
Tai would offer to work for Mike for free
in return for Mike teaching him what he knows

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:02.510
and Mike would agree.

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:06.890
And this is what would eventually lead to
Tai starting his first online business in

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:10.060
2007: Elite Global Dating LLC.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:15.190
As the name suggests, this was a dating website,
and honestly, Tai was actually way ahead of

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:16.190
his time.

00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:20.860
Tinder, for example, wasn’t even started
till 2012 and today it pulls in well above

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:22.890
a billion dollars per year in revenue.

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:27.340
But, maybe, Tai was a bit too early as he
struggled to garner very many users.

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:30.530
Tai wasn’t willing to quit though and it
didn’t take him long to switch to sketchy

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:33.610
strategies atleast according to allegations.

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:38.040
The story goes that Tai created and ran a
bunch of fake profiles with pictures of attractive

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:39.040
females.

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:43.690
While some people saw through it and left
terrible reviews, many simps fell for it.

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:48.720
And you just have to take one look at a popular
website today to know just how lucrative this

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:49.860
can be.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:53.780
Tai would use the money that he made off this
website to start a nightclub in hollywood

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:56.840
and before you knew it, he was actually pretty
well off.

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:01.250
He could’ve called it a day right here,
but it was clear to Tai that the real opportunity

00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:02.250
was the internet.

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:06.849
So, it mid 2014, he started posting on his
YouTube channel about the books he was reading

00:04:06.849 --> 00:04:08.470
and the main takeaways.

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:12.269
He posted about a dozen of these, but none
of them really gained much traction.

00:04:12.269 --> 00:04:17.860
So, in early 2015, he would give a Ted talk
to hopefully grow a larger audience.

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:21.820
As you might’ve guessed, the Ted Talk was
about reading books as well.

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:25.910
This definitely gave him a push forward, but
the real leap didn’t come till summer of

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:31.240
2015 when he decided to film a video in his
garage and run paid ads.

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:36.440
This ad basically became ubiquitous across
YouTube, and while many were complaining,

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:39.910
some went down the Tai Lopez rabbit hole and
that’s all he needed.

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:44.789
Over the next few years, Tai would solidify
his own credibility by collaborating with

00:04:44.789 --> 00:04:46.410
already credible people.

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:50.760
For example, he had Michael Jordan’s trainer
Tim Grover on the channel and he even had

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:52.789
Mark Cuban on the channel.

00:04:52.789 --> 00:04:56.670
Aside from these credibility establishing
videos, Tai also posted a bunch of videos

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:59.370
that casually showed of his lifestyle.

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:03.669
Sometimes he was at casinos, sometimes he
was in supercars, and othertimes he was just

00:05:03.669 --> 00:05:05.479
walking around his mansion.

00:05:05.479 --> 00:05:09.940
But, regardless of the background, a few things
were constant which were the crappy production

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:13.020
quality and his apparent indifference to the
situation.

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:17.720
You would usually never associate this attributes
with successful ad campaigns, but this was

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:19.340
Tai’s secret formula.

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:23.389
These attributes made it seem like Tai wasn’t
trying to sell you anything and that he was

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:25.650
genuinely just sharing his thoughts and wisdom.

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:31.390
All of this would build up to his second most
iconic video of all time: here in my backyard.

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:35.830
This video wasn’t all that different from
any of his other videos, it pretty much followed

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:36.832
the exact same formula.

00:05:36.832 --> 00:05:38.070
But, the reason this video is so important
is not because it was super popular.

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:42.449
No, the reason this video is so important
is because people with power would start questioning

00:05:42.449 --> 00:05:46.440
Tai’s credibility for the first time.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:50.410
EXPOSED:
Likely the biggest channel that called out

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:55.380
Tai was h3h3 productions who was able to find
Tai’s house on Zillow.

00:05:55.380 --> 00:06:00.539
Now technically, the listing does say off
market, but Ethan from h3h3 would describe

00:06:00.539 --> 00:06:04.880
Zillow as a short term rental website and
basically allege that Tai was just renting

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:06.750
this house for his videos.

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:12.360
And given that this video got over 6 million
views, it led to quite a bit of drama.

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:16.930
More people would start looking into Tai and
the more they looked the more they realized

00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:22.180
that Tai basically had no background or reputation
other than the one that he portrayed on his

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:23.419
YouTube channel.

00:06:23.419 --> 00:06:27.630
For obvious reasons, this made people quite
suspicious.

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:31.759
Tai would go onto address this video head
on by inviting Ethan to his quote on quote

00:06:31.759 --> 00:06:32.979
rented house.

00:06:32.979 --> 00:06:37.500
Tai would give him a tour of the house and
show him his supercars and whatnot.

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:42.660
Tai was probably hoping that this confrontation
would give him more credibility, and to his

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:45.390
credit, he did explain himself pretty well.

00:06:45.390 --> 00:06:49.080
He came clean about how he didn’t own many
of the things he showed off like his house

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:52.120
and his cars and that he leased them instead.

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:56.250
He also clarified that he leased these items
not because he couldn’t afford them but

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:59.070
because it made more financial sense to lease.

00:06:59.070 --> 00:07:01.180
And there is truth to this.

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:05.560
It makes no sense to sink a quarter million
into a lambhorghini or $15 million into a

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:10.820
mansion when you can invest this money into
things like Google, Apple, Tesla, the S&amp;P

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:15.569
500, or your own business which would all
likely generate far higher returns.

00:07:15.569 --> 00:07:20.400
So, while Tai did have a good explanation
as to why he leased, people didn’t exactly

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:26.120
trust him and it wasn’t because his explanation
was bad or that people suspected that he wasn’t

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:27.300
actually rich.

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:31.639
Honestly, his wealth never really should’ve
been a question from the start.

00:07:31.639 --> 00:07:35.760
To run the type of ad campaign that Tai ran,
you need millions of dollars.

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:40.660
So, he either had this money himself or he
had access to investors who had this money.

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:46.360
Both of these scenarios point to Tai being
rich himself, so that was never really the

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:47.360
question.

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:51.240
The real question was whether this guy could
be trusted, and this confrontation made it

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:54.610
clear that the answer to that question was
a hard no.

00:07:54.610 --> 00:07:58.770
Throughout the house tour, we would see a
dozen or so attorneys, bodyguards, investors,

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:03.490
or whoever they were monitor each and every
interaction and this just raised a bunch of

00:08:03.490 --> 00:08:04.680
red flags.

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:09.569
Pretty quickly, Tai went from being rich and
genuine to rich and shady and more people

00:08:09.569 --> 00:08:14.900
started to see Tai’s ads for what they really
were, not authentic life advice, but brilliantly

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:15.979
designed ads.

00:08:15.979 --> 00:08:20.759
And given that Tai’s entire brand image
was built around his apparent authenticity,

00:08:20.759 --> 00:08:25.060
as this disappeared from the equation, so
did Tai’s prominence and viewership.

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:28.070
This isn’t to say that Tai was doing bad
financially though.

00:08:28.070 --> 00:08:32.219
Tai had already convinced a large portion
of his viewers that he was some sort of genius

00:08:32.219 --> 00:08:37.310
mentor, and these guys would continue to buy
Tai’s new courses making him even richer.

00:08:37.310 --> 00:08:42.099
So, while Tai’s internet popularity was
falling off a cliff, the same could not be

00:08:42.099 --> 00:08:44.540
said about his net worth.

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:50.390
TAI REBORN:
All of this brings us to where we are today.

00:08:50.390 --> 00:08:54.570
For the past few years, Tai has primarily
been marketing to people who have already

00:08:54.570 --> 00:09:00.329
bought into the Tai Lopez brand, but he has
struggled to bring in new people until recently

00:09:00.329 --> 00:09:03.519
and that’s all thanks to YouTube shorts.

00:09:03.519 --> 00:09:07.510
Tai is by no means a new player when it comes
to short form content.

00:09:07.510 --> 00:09:12.240
Usually, he’s very quick when it comes to
jumping onto new trends whether it be SnapChat,

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:14.930
Instagram, TikTok, or YouTube Shorts.

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:19.829
But, Tai wasn’t gaining much traction with
his regular just trust me bro advice.

00:09:19.829 --> 00:09:25.459
So, he started experimenting with some other
formats which led him to a goldmine: feel

00:09:25.459 --> 00:09:26.459
good shorts.

00:09:26.459 --> 00:09:31.320
Usually, this consists of soldiers returning
home, acts of kindness, and anything is possible

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:33.089
type motivation.

00:09:33.089 --> 00:09:37.310
And let’s just say these shorts are exploding
in popularity.

00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:41.590
Tai’s most popular short literally has a
hundred million views and he has dozens of

00:09:41.590 --> 00:09:45.670
shorts racking in millions if not tens of
millions of views each.

00:09:45.670 --> 00:09:50.079
More times than not, I don’t think the viewers
of these shorts even realize who’s posting

00:09:50.079 --> 00:09:51.079
them.

00:09:51.079 --> 00:09:56.260
And even if they read the name, I don't think
they actually know who Tai Lopez even is.

00:09:56.260 --> 00:10:01.950
This is extremely powerful because it basically
gives Tai a brand new slate to work with.

00:10:01.950 --> 00:10:06.010
These people don’t know anything about his
background and they just know that he posts

00:10:06.010 --> 00:10:07.060
feel good videos.

00:10:07.060 --> 00:10:11.000
If they start following him because of these
shorts, they’ll eventually be served shorts

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:14.649
about his business advice leading to more
sales for his courses.

00:10:14.649 --> 00:10:19.010
And the best part about all of this for Tai
is that he doesn’t even have to pay for

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:20.410
the exposure.

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:24.140
All of these views are completely organic
and they’re getting more views than his

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:25.329
paid ads.

00:10:25.329 --> 00:10:29.740
So far, I haven’t really seen anybody on
YouTube notice this or really point this out.

00:10:29.740 --> 00:10:33.700
But, if Tai keeps getting this number of views,
it’s just a matter of time until someone

00:10:33.700 --> 00:10:36.139
starts exposing him all over again.

00:10:36.139 --> 00:10:38.160
I’m looking at CoffeeZilla here.

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:44.040
But, in the meantime, Tai Lopez is very much
back and he’s bigger than ever.

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:49.940
THE TRUTH ABOUT TAI LOPEZ:
At the end of the day, many people like to

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:52.880
criticize Tai and call him a scammer.

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:58.200
And yes, the bottomline is that he does sell
overpriced courses mainly consisting of content

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:00.410
that you can find for free on the internet.

00:11:00.410 --> 00:11:03.709
But, that doesn’t mean that there’s nothing
to learn from him.

00:11:03.709 --> 00:11:07.990
The learning opportunity with Tai is not his
courses or whatever else he’s selling.

00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:12.510
The real learning opportunity with Tai is
his marketing, his story telling, and his

00:11:12.510 --> 00:11:13.510
persuasion.

00:11:13.510 --> 00:11:18.540
Now, I’m not recommending that you go sell
online courses yourself, but maybe you could

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:21.450
apply some of his strategies into your own
marketing efforts.

00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:26.180
If you’re doing a job interview, how do
come off as authentic and confident as opposed

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:28.110
to rehearsed and nervous.

00:11:28.110 --> 00:11:32.200
If you own a business, how do you create brand
image that feel genuine and resonates with

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:34.810
your target market.

00:11:34.810 --> 00:11:40.829
So, as Tai goes through this second wave of
popularity, you shouldn’t buy his courses.

00:11:40.829 --> 00:11:47.709
But, you should pay close attention to why
Tai’s winning but that’s just what I think.

00:11:47.709 --> 00:11:53.030
Do you think Tai Lopez is a scammer, a marketing
genius, or some combination of both?

